  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 204 PM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The forecast challenge this weekend is evolution of \nclosed mid/upper lvl cyclonic circulation and track of sfc low pres \nand associated pops and qpf placement. Sfc analysis shows stationary \nboundary draped along the Eastern Seaboard with axis of showers and \nembedded thunderstorms. Meanwhile, GOES-19 water vapor imagery shows \na potent/compact closed cyclonic circulation over the central Great \nLakes this aftn with north side deformation/moisture over the \nnorthern Lakes. This circulation is progged to move southeast toward \nthe Southern Tier of NY by 00z Sunday, while north side deformation \nand mid lvl moisture axis lifting from southwest to northeast acrs \nour cwa on Sat aftn. The mid/upper lvl circulation tries to capture \nsfc low pres by late Sat into Sunday with weak inverted sfc trof \ndeveloping acrs our cwa. The combination of weak sfc convergence and \nmid lvl deformation on north/northwest side of closed 850-700mb \ncyclonic circulation supports high categorical (85-100%) Sat aft \ninto Sunday acrs most of our cwa. As 1000mb sfc low pres lifts acrs \neastern Maine on Sunday brisk/northwest upslope flow develops under \nmodest low level caa. It should be noted the airmass behind this \nsystem is only marginally cool enough to support summit lvls snow, \ngiven progged 850mb temps near 0C and 925mb of 1C to 3C. Any snow \naccumulations wl be above 3000 ft and generally a dusting to 1 inch, \nmaybe 1 to 3 inches above 4000 ft acrs the High Peaks in Essex \nCounty, NY. Rainfall amounts will be 0.30\" to 0.60\" with localized \nhigher amounts possible in favorable upslope areas of the northern \nDacks and central/northern Greens. This general idea of higher pops \nand qpf is supported by the latest AI-GFS/AI-ECWMF 12z/06z runs\nrespectively. Much cooler temps wl be expected this weekend, \nespecially on Sunday with brisk northwest winds/damp conditions \nand highs only in the 40s. \n\nNext potent s/w energy in the progressive northwest flow aloft \narrives on Monday with additional widespread precip likely, \nespecially favorable upslope areas. Progged thickness values are \ncooler for this system, along with progged 850mb temps btwn -1C \nand - 3C, supporting slightly lower snow levels. However, still\nsome uncertainty on amount of moisture in nw flow aloft with pw\nvalues in the 0.30 and 0.40 range and limited pocket of mid lvl\nmoisture. For now have kept close to NBM pops/qpf and thermal \nprofiles, but expect changes to occur as it becomes a near term \nforecast focus. Highs Monday wl range from the mid 30s to upper \n40s acrs our region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The large scale cyclonic gyre over the Hudson Bay will \ncontinue to eject several shortwaves through mid to late next week. \nTuesday and Wednesday will see shower chances focused in the higher \nterrain, with perhaps some blocking against the Greens pushing \nmoisture into the Champlain Valley either day. The most robust of \nthe two days looks to be Tuesday, with a more defined vort max \ntracking near the International Border. Thermal profiles Tuesday \ninto Wednesday would be supportive of some nighttime snow potential \nin the higher terrain upslope favored regions above 2500ft, with \nlight accumulations possible given the prolonged period of 925-850mb \ntemperatures at or below 0 through mid week. Below 2500ft, mainly \nrain is to be expected, with little in the way of impacts. Should \nMount Mansfield still be holding onto the latest 1\" snowfall, of \nwhich the record is November 9th, it appears currently that we will \nhave a high chance to reach 1\" at the latest by Wednesday morning. \nBy the late week, ensembles are coming into better agreement that a \nmid/upper level closed low will develop and swing through the area. \nThis would point towards sharply cooler daytime highs as 850mb \ntemperatures will fall to near -5C, supporting highs in the upper \n30s to low 40s across the area Thursday, and especially Friday, with \nsome chances for some light shower activity.",
